      <!-- Intro -->
      <p>
        Polyurethane sealant formulation represents one of the most technically demanding segments of the adhesives and sealants industry — a class of materials that must simultaneously bond to dissimilar substrates, elongate under cyclic stress, recover elastically, and survive decades of temperature cycling, UV radiation, and chemical exposure in some of the most demanding joint environments in construction and manufacturing. Unlike rigid sealants or mastics, polyurethane sealants are true elastomers: they accommodate joint movement, absorb mechanical energy without transmitting load, and return to their original dimensions when stress is released. This profile makes them the primary choice for expansion joints in building facades, concrete floors, precast panels, and industrial structures worldwide.
      </p>

      <!-- ─── SECTION 1 ─── -->
      <section id="why-pu">
        <h2>Why Polyurethane Dominates Construction Joint Sealing</h2>

        <p>
          The case for polyurethane in joint sealing rests on a combination of mechanical properties unavailable in any single competing chemistry. Silicone sealants offer superior UV stability and high-temperature performance, but they are unpaintable and exhibit relatively poor abrasion resistance. Acrylic sealants are economical but are limited to low-movement joints and degrade under sustained water immersion. Polysulphide sealants have largely been displaced from new construction. Polyurethane addresses the majority of movement joint requirements — paintability, abrasion resistance, good adhesion to porous substrates, and movement accommodation up to ±50% — in a single chemistry platform.
        </p>

        <p>
          The chemistry foundation is the urethane linkage, formed when an isocyanate group (–N=C=O) reacts with a hydroxyl group (–OH) to yield –NH–CO–O–. As described in the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polyurethane" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikipedia article on polyurethane</a>, this reaction is exothermic and proceeds readily at room temperature, particularly when catalysed by organotin or tertiary amine catalysts. In sealant practice, the isocyanate and polyol are not combined in one shot — instead, the manufacturing process first produces an isocyanate-terminated prepolymer: a controlled intermediate created by reacting a polyol (typically polypropylene glycol, molecular weight 2000–6000 Da) with a stoichiometric excess of isocyanate to yield a chain-extended molecule capped with terminal –NCO groups. It is this prepolymer architecture that controls the final sealant's modulus, elongation, and elastic recovery — far more precisely than any one-step formulation approach.
        </p>

        <p>
          For the wider context of adhesive and bonding technologies across polymer chemistries, our <a href="/adhesives-and-sealants" target="_top">adhesives and sealants</a> technical resource provides a comprehensive overview of the full spectrum of structural and elastic bonding systems used in modern construction and manufacturing.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ─── SECTION 2 ─── -->
      <section id="moisture-cure">
        <h2>One-Component Moisture-Cure PU Sealant Chemistry</h2>

        <p>
          In a one-component polyurethane sealant, the curing reaction is driven entirely by water vapour present in the surrounding atmosphere. The prepolymer is packaged in a sealed, dry cartridge — any contact with moisture during storage would prematurely crosslink the product. Once applied to the joint and exposed to air, atmospheric humidity initiates curing through a two-step mechanism at the isocyanate-terminated chain ends.
        </p>

        <p>
          In the first step, a terminal isocyanate group (–NCO) reacts with a water molecule (H₂O) to form an unstable carbamic acid intermediate (R–NH–COOH). This decomposes spontaneously, releasing carbon dioxide (CO₂) gas and generating a primary amine (R–NH₂). In the second step, the free amine reacts rapidly with another –NCO group from the prepolymer chain to form a urea crosslink (–NH–CO–NH–). These urea bridges between prepolymer chains constitute the primary crosslinked network of the cured sealant, responsible for the elastic modulus, elongation, and recovery properties measured in service.
        </p>

        <p>
          The CO₂ generated in the first step must diffuse out through the forming sealant skin before gel point is reached. If the surface skins prematurely — particularly at high temperature and high relative humidity — CO₂ becomes trapped, causing internal blistering and voids that significantly reduce mechanical performance and adhesion. Formulators manage this risk through careful selection of plasticiser (diisononyl phthalate, DINP, or dioctyl terephthalate, DOTP), filler particle size (calcium carbonate, carbon black, precipitated silica), and catalyst level. Cure depth advances from the exposed surface inward at approximately 3–4 mm per day at 23°C and 50% relative humidity; full mechanical properties are reached at 7 days under standard conditions. Below 5°C or below 20% relative humidity, cure rate drops substantially, which can make 2K systems the more practical choice. For a full treatment of adhesive and sealant technologies, see our <a href="/adhesives-and-sealants-formulations-and-technology" target="_top">adhesives and sealants formulations guide</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Diagram -->
      <div class="article-img-wrap" style="margin: 2em 0 0.6em; border-radius: var(--pu-radius); overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid var(--pu-border);">
        <img 
          src={`https://cdn.globalformulation.com/images/${slug}/${slug}-diagram.webp`}
