      <!-- ─── SECTION 4 ─── -->
      <section id="joint-design">
        <h2>Construction Joint Sealant Design and Adhesion</h2>

        <p>
          Joint design is the engineering discipline that translates a sealant's rated performance into a serviceable, durable joint — and it is where most sealant failures in practice originate, independent of the sealant's own formulation quality. The key US standard governing elastomeric joint sealant classification and performance is <a href="https://www.astm.org/c0920-22.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM C920</a>, which defines movement accommodation classes as ±12.5%, ±20%, ±25%, and ±50% of the original joint width. A sealant designated Class ±25, applied to a 20 mm wide joint, must survive cyclic movement between 15 mm (25% compression) and 25 mm (25% extension) without adhesive or cohesive failure over the specified test cycle count.
        </p>

        <p>
          Two joint geometry rules are fundamental. First, joint width must be sized to accommodate the anticipated movement: width equals the maximum movement divided by the movement class factor, multiplied by a safety factor of 1.3–1.5. For thermal expansion joints in concrete or masonry facades, this calculation uses the substrate's coefficient of thermal expansion, the anticipated service temperature range, and the panel length. Second, joint depth must be controlled using a backing rod (closed-cell polyethylene foam backer) to achieve the recommended width:depth ratio of 2:1. The backing rod also prevents the sealant from bonding to the back wall of the joint. Three-face bonding — where the sealant adheres to both faces and the back wall — severely restricts the sealant's ability to deform uniformly as the joint opens, concentrating all strain at the adhesion interfaces and causing adhesive failure at a fraction of the rated movement class.
        </p>

        <p>
          Surface preparation determines adhesion quality. On concrete and masonry, substrates must be free from form release agents, curing compounds, dust, and efflorescence before primer application. Most PU sealants require an isocyanate-based primer on cementitious surfaces to achieve the adhesion values specified under ASTM C794. On metal and glass, solvent wiping to remove contamination followed by the sealant manufacturer's adhesion promoter is standard practice.
        </p>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Key Insight</strong>
          Joint sizing and backing rod placement determine whether a correctly formulated sealant succeeds or fails in service. An undersized joint forces the sealant beyond its rated movement capacity; a three-face bonded joint concentrates all strain at the adhesion interfaces regardless of sealant quality or primer use.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Infographic -->
      <div class="article-img-wrap" style="margin: 2em 0 0.6em; border-radius: var(--pu-radius); overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid var(--pu-border);">
