      <!-- ─── SECTION 5 ─── -->
      <section id="weathering">
        <h2>Weathering, UV Stability, and Failure Modes</h2>

        <p>
          The most significant weathering limitation of standard polyurethane sealants is UV-induced photooxidative degradation. Aromatic PU systems — based on MDI (4,4'-methylene diphenyl diisocyanate) or TDI (toluene diisocyanate) prepolymers — undergo a well-documented degradation pathway under UV exposure: the aromatic methylene and aromatic ring structures adjacent to the urethane linkage absorb UV photons and form highly coloured quinone-imine and azomethine intermediates, producing the characteristic progressive yellowing. As photooxidation continues, the surface loses polymer chain integrity, generating a friable chalk layer. This degradation is primarily superficial in the short term — the bulk elastomer may retain useful mechanical properties while the surface oxidises — but long-term UV exposure progressively reduces elongation at break and adhesion strength across the entire cross-section.
        </p>

        <p>
          Aliphatic polyurethane systems — based on IPDI (isophorone diisocyanate) or HDI (hexamethylene diisocyanate) prepolymers — lack the UV-absorbing aromatic ring structure and retain colour and surface integrity under prolonged outdoor UV exposure. Aliphatic prepolymers carry a significant cost premium over aromatic equivalents and are the specified chemistry for visible facade joints, curtain wall perimeter sealants, and any application where colour stability is a performance requirement without paint coverage. The environmental considerations associated with isocyanate-containing sealants — including VOC content of solvent-based formulations and MDI vapour control — connect to the broader regulatory framework covered in our article on <a href="/volatile-organic-compounds-and-pollution" target="_top">VOCs and industrial pollution</a>.
        </p>

        <div class="callout callout-amber">
          <strong>Rule of Thumb</strong>
          Standard aromatic polyurethane sealants (MDI or TDI-based) will yellow and chalk on permanently exposed facades, regardless of product quality. Specify aliphatic PU (IPDI or HDI prepolymer base) for any joint that will remain unpainted or subject to direct UV exposure over its service life.
        </div>

        <p>Common failure modes in polyurethane sealant installations:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Adhesive failure</strong> — debonding at the sealant–substrate interface; caused by inadequate surface preparation, primer omission, incompatible substrates, or movement beyond the rated class</li>
          <li><strong>Cohesive failure</strong> — tearing within the sealant body; indicates joint undersizing, wrong movement class selection, or aged sealant with reduced elongation capacity</li>
          <li><strong>Compression set</strong> — failure to recover original dimensions after sustained compression; indicates plasticiser migration to the joint interface or thermal degradation of the polymer network</li>
          <li><strong>Surface cracking</strong> — network of fine surface cracks without through-failure; typically indicates UV degradation in aromatic systems or inadequate modulus for the movement demand</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- ─── SECTION 6 ─── -->
      <section id="applications">
        <h2>Construction and Industrial Applications</h2>

        <p>
          Polyurethane sealants serve as the primary joint sealing material across a wide range of construction and industrial sectors, each with specific performance requirements that the 1K/2K chemistries address in different ways. In building facades, they fill the expansion joints between cladding panels, precast concrete elements, and curtain wall frames — joints sized to absorb the thermal expansion of large structures without transmitting load to adjacent components. Floor joints in industrial facilities, warehouses, logistics centres, and car parks require traffic-rated sealants (ASTM C920, Use T) that resist wheel load abrasion, fork-lift traffic, and chemical contamination from cleaning agents and fuels, demanding the higher hardness and abrasion resistance achievable with 2K formulations.
        </p>

        <p>
          Precast concrete construction relies on PU sealants to seal perimeter joints between individual panels, providing weather resistance while accommodating inter-panel movement from wind loading, differential settlement, and thermal cycling. Sanitary applications — commercial kitchens, food processing facilities, pharmaceutical cleanrooms — use low-modulus PU formulations where substrate movement from wet/dry cycling is continuous. Here, polyurethane competes with neutral-cure silicone; the decision typically hinges on paintability (where PU is preferred) versus fungal resistance and high-temperature performance (where silicone is preferred). Automotive body seam sealing represents another substantial application, though this segment increasingly uses MS polymer (modified silicone polymer) hybrid systems that combine PU adhesion with silicone UV stability. Our comprehensive <a href="/adhesives-and-sealants-formulations-and-technology" target="_top">adhesives and sealants formulations and technology</a> resource covers MS polymer systems and the full range of bonding and sealing technologies in detail.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ─── FAQ ─── -->
      <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is the key difference between a one-component and two-component polyurethane sealant?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Both 1K and 2K polyurethane sealants are based on isocyanate-terminated prepolymers, but their crosslinking mechanisms differ fundamentally. In a 1K system, the prepolymer is packaged in a sealed, moisture-free cartridge and cures after application through reaction with atmospheric water vapour. Water reacts with terminal isocyanate groups (–NCO) in two steps: first generating an unstable carbamic acid that decomposes to an amine and CO₂, then the amine reacts with a second –NCO group to form a urea crosslink. The CO₂ is released through the sealant surface during cure. A 2K system eliminates moisture dependency: Part A contains the NCO prepolymer and Part B contains a curative package of polyol, amine, or both, plus catalyst. When mixed, Parts A and B react directly to form urethane and urea crosslinks without gas evolution, giving faster, more predictable cure and enabling use in low-humidity environments.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What does the ASTM C920 classification mean for polyurethane sealants?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">ASTM C920 is the primary US standard for elastomeric joint sealants. Its code describes four parameters: Type (S = single-component, M = multicomponent), Grade (P = pourable/self-levelling, NS = non-sag), Class (movement accommodation: ±12.5%, ±20%, ±25%, or ±50% of joint width), and Use (G = glazing, A = non-traffic, T = traffic, I = immersion, O = other). A designation of M-NS-25 describes a two-component, non-sag sealant rated for ±25% cyclic movement. These ratings are verified by cyclic movement testing — not estimated from modulus data. Joint designers should select the class based on calculated movement demand plus a safety factor of 1.3–1.5.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why do polyurethane sealants turn yellow or chalky when exposed to sunlight?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Standard polyurethane sealants use aromatic isocyanates — MDI or TDI — in their prepolymer base. The aromatic ring adjacent to the urethane linkage absorbs UV radiation and undergoes photooxidation, forming coloured quinone-imine and azomethine structures that produce progressive yellowing, followed by surface chalking as polymer chain integrity is lost. This is primarily a surface phenomenon initially; the bulk joint may remain mechanically functional for years. Aliphatic isocyanate systems (IPDI or HDI-based prepolymers) lack this UV-absorbing aromatic structure and retain colour and surface integrity under long-term outdoor exposure, making them essential for permanently exposed, unpainted facade joints.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What primer should be used with a polyurethane sealant on concrete?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Most polyurethane sealants require a dedicated primer on porous substrates such as concrete, masonry, and natural stone to achieve adequate adhesion under cyclic movement. Isocyanate-based primers are most common for cementitious substrates — they react with silanol groups on the concrete surface and with surface moisture to form a urethane-type bond. Primer selection must be confirmed by the sealant manufacturer: a mismatched primer can act as a release agent rather than an adhesion promoter. On dense, non-porous substrates (glass, aluminium, polished concrete), a solvent wipe and the manufacturer's adhesion promoter are typically required. Compatibility testing should always be performed before committing to a sealant system for critical facade or structural joints.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How does a polyurethane sealant differ from a silicone sealant?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Polyurethane and silicone are the two dominant elastomeric sealant chemistries in construction with distinct profiles. Polyurethane offers better abrasion resistance, is paintable after cure (silicone repels most paints), and achieves strong adhesion to porous substrates without specialist primers. However, aromatic PU systems yellow under UV, and most PU sealants have a service temperature ceiling of approximately +90°C versus silicone's +200°C. Silicone is inherently UV stable, delivers outstanding high-temperature performance, and is preferred for structural glazing applications. Polyurethane is favoured for paintable construction joints, floor joints, and traffic-bearing applications. For sanitary joints, the choice depends on whether paintability or fungal resistance is the primary criterion.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is the maximum joint movement a polyurethane sealant can accommodate?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Under ASTM C920 Class ±50 (the highest standard classification), a polyurethane sealant accommodates movement equal to ±50% of the original joint width in repeated cycles. Most construction-grade PU sealants are rated ±25%. A 20 mm wide joint rated at ±25% can cycle between 15 mm (compression) and 25 mm (extension) without failure. Achieving this rating in service requires the width:depth ratio to be maintained at 2:1 to 3:1 and the sealant to bond on two faces only — a backing rod prevents three-face bonding that would concentrate all strain at the adhesion lines. Joint width should be calculated from the substrate's thermal expansion coefficient, the service temperature range, and a safety factor of 1.3–1.5.</div>
        </details>
      </section>

